---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MortgageRecastCalculator from "../../../components/MortgageRecastCalculator.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="UK Mortgage Recast & Overpayment Calculator | Overpayment vs Remortgage"
  description="Explore UK mortgage overpayment scenarios and model monthly payment reductions when lenders recalculate terms after lump-sum overpayments."
  ogImage="https://taxdebtwealthsuite.com/images/og-mortgage-recast-uk.png"
>
  <link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://taxdebtwealthsuite.com/mortgage-recast-calculator/" slot="head" />
  <link rel="alternate" hreflang="en-us" href="https://taxdebtwealthsuite.com/mortgage-recast-calculator/" slot="head" />
  <link rel="alternate" hreflang="en-ca" href="https://taxdebtwealthsuite.com/mortgage-recast-calculator/canada/" slot="head" />
  <link rel="alternate" hreflang="en-gb" href="https://taxdebtwealthsuite.com/mortgage-recast-calculator/uk/" slot="head" />
  <link rel="alternate" hreflang="en-au" href="https://taxdebtwealthsuite.com/mortgage-recast-calculator/australia/" slot="head" />

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline slot="head">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@graph": [
      {
        "@type": "FinanceApplication",
        "name": "UK Mortgage Overpayment & Recast Calculator",
        "applicationCategory": "FinanceApplication",
        "operatingSystem": "Web",
        "provider": {
          "@id": "https://taxdebtwealthsuite.com/#organization"
        },
        "offers": {
          "@type": "Offer",
          "price": "0",
          "priceCurrency": "GBP"
        },
        "description": "Model monthly payment reductions following UK mortgage overpayments and lump-sum restructuring."
      },
      {
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Does the UK have mortgage recasting?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Formal 'recasting' is rare in the UK. UK borrowers usually make overpayments (typically up to 10% annually without Early Repayment Charges) or adjust terms during remortgaging."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "How much can I overpay on my UK mortgage without penalty?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Most UK fixed-rate and tracker mortgage deals allow overpayments of up to 10% of the outstanding balance per year without triggering an Early Repayment Charge (ERC). Some lenders set this at 10% of the original loan amount. Check your mortgage offer document or contact your lender to confirm your specific overpayment allowance."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What is the difference between overpaying and remortgaging in the UK?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Overpaying means making extra payments on your existing mortgage to reduce the balance faster, which can shorten the term or lower future payments when the lender recalculates. Remortgaging means switching to an entirely new mortgage deal — potentially with a different lender, rate, and term — which involves valuation fees, legal costs, and a new affordability assessment but can secure a lower interest rate."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

  <main class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
    <div class="mb-8 text-center sm:text-left">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight font-sans">
        UK Mortgage Recast & Overpayment Calculator
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-400 max-w-3xl">
        Evaluate UK mortgage overpayment strategies, compare remortgaging options, and model monthly payment reductions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <MortgageRecastCalculator country="uk" />

    <!-- Explanatory Content Wrapper -->
    <div class="mt-12 space-y-8 text-slate-300">
      <!-- Introduction Section -->
      <section class="glass-panel p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl space-y-4">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-[#00F5D4]/10 text-[#00F5D4] text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider border border-[#00F5D4]/20">
          <span>UK Mortgage Framework</span>
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">Introduction</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          "Mortgage recasting" as a named product does not exist in the UK mortgage market. In the United States, a recast lets a borrower pay a lump sum to reduce the principal while keeping the same rate and term, with the lender recalculating lower monthly payments — but UK lenders don't offer this as a formal feature. Instead, UK borrowers have two primary mechanisms to achieve a similar outcome: <strong>mortgage overpayments</strong>, where you pay more than the required monthly amount (most deals allow up to 10% of the outstanding balance per year without triggering an Early Repayment Charge), and <strong>remortgaging</strong>, where you switch to an entirely new deal — potentially with a different lender, rate, and term. This calculator models both of these UK-specific strategies so you can compare the interest savings and monthly payment reductions of making lump-sum overpayments on your current deal versus the costs and benefits of remortgaging to a new product.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Key Benefits Section -->
      <section class="glass-panel p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl space-y-4">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-[#7B61FF]/10 text-[#7B61FF] text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider border border-[#7B61FF]/20">
          <span>Strategic Value</span>
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">Key Benefits</h2>
        <ul class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 pt-2">
          <li class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="text-[#00F5D4] font-bold mt-0.5">•</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-white block mb-1">Model Overpayment Impact</strong>
              <span class="text-sm text-slate-400">See how lump-sum or regular overpayments reduce your outstanding balance and shorten your mortgage term or lower future payments.</span>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="text-[#00F5D4] font-bold mt-0.5">•</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-white block mb-1">Quantify Interest Savings</strong>
              <span class="text-sm text-slate-400">Calculate total interest saved over the remaining mortgage term by applying a lump-sum overpayment within your penalty-free allowance.</span>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="text-[#00F5D4] font-bold mt-0.5">•</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-white block mb-1">Compare Overpayment vs. Remortgage</strong>
              <span class="text-sm text-slate-400">Weigh the cost-free simplicity of staying on your current deal with overpayments against the potential rate savings (minus fees) of switching lenders.</span>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="text-[#00F5D4] font-bold mt-0.5">•</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-white block mb-1">Avoid Early Repayment Charges</strong>
              <span class="text-sm text-slate-400">Understand the 10% annual overpayment cap on most UK fixed-rate deals so you can maximise savings without triggering ERCs.</span>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="text-[#00F5D4] font-bold mt-0.5">•</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-white block mb-1">Plan Term Reduction</strong>
              <span class="text-sm text-slate-400">Estimate how many months or years sooner you can become mortgage-free by redirecting spare cash into overpayments.</span>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <section class="glass-panel p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl space-y-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-[#00F5D4]/10 text-[#00F5D4] text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider border border-[#00F5D4]/20">
          <span>Common Questions</span>
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] overflow-hidden">
            <button 
              type="button"
              onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden'); this.querySelector('svg').classList.toggle('rotate-180');" 
              class="w-full p-4 sm:p-5 text-left font-semibold text-white flex items-center justify-between gap-4 hover:bg-slate-900/50 transition-colors focus:outline-none"
            >
              <span>Does the UK have mortgage recasting?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#00F5D4] shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path>
              </svg>
            </button>
            <div class="hidden p-4 sm:p-5 pt-0 text-sm text-slate-300 border-t border-[#1E294B]/50 leading-relaxed">
              Formal "recasting" is rare in the UK. UK borrowers usually make overpayments (typically up to 10% annually without Early Repayment Charges) or adjust terms during remortgaging.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] overflow-hidden">
            <button 
              type="button"
              onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden'); this.querySelector('svg').classList.toggle('rotate-180');" 
              class="w-full p-4 sm:p-5 text-left font-semibold text-white flex items-center justify-between gap-4 hover:bg-slate-900/50 transition-colors focus:outline-none"
            >
              <span>How much can I overpay on my UK mortgage without penalty?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#00F5D4] shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path>
              </svg>
            </button>
            <div class="hidden p-4 sm:p-5 pt-0 text-sm text-slate-300 border-t border-[#1E294B]/50 leading-relaxed">
              Most UK fixed-rate and tracker mortgage deals allow overpayments of up to 10% of the outstanding balance per year without triggering an Early Repayment Charge (ERC). Some lenders set this at 10% of the original loan amount. Check your mortgage offer document or contact your lender to confirm your specific overpayment allowance.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] overflow-hidden">
            <button 
              type="button"
              onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden'); this.querySelector('svg').classList.toggle('rotate-180');" 
              class="w-full p-4 sm:p-5 text-left font-semibold text-white flex items-center justify-between gap-4 hover:bg-slate-900/50 transition-colors focus:outline-none"
            >
              <span>What is the difference between overpaying and remortgaging in the UK?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#00F5D4] shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path>
              </svg>
            </button>
            <div class="hidden p-4 sm:p-5 pt-0 text-sm text-slate-300 border-t border-[#1E294B]/50 leading-relaxed">
              Overpaying means making extra payments on your existing mortgage to reduce the balance faster, which can shorten the term or lower future payments when the lender recalculates. Remortgaging means switching to an entirely new mortgage deal — potentially with a different lender, rate, and term — which involves valuation fees, legal costs, and a new affordability assessment but can secure a lower interest rate.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Methodology Section -->
      <section class="glass-panel p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl space-y-4">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-[#7B61FF]/10 text-[#7B61FF] text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider border border-[#7B61FF]/20">
          <span>Calculation Logic</span>
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">Methodology</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            This calculator uses standard amortisation mathematics to model the effect of a lump-sum overpayment on a UK repayment mortgage. It takes your current outstanding balance, remaining term, and interest rate, then computes the standard monthly payment. After deducting the lump-sum overpayment from the principal, it re-amortises the reduced balance over the same remaining term to show how much your monthly payment decreases — or, alternatively, how many months sooner you could clear the mortgage by keeping your payment the same.
          </p>
          <p>
            Interest savings are calculated by comparing total interest paid under the original schedule against total interest under the overpayment scenario. The model assumes a fixed interest rate for the remaining term; if you are on a variable or tracker rate, the results represent a snapshot at the current rate. Early Repayment Charges are not automatically deducted — confirm with your lender whether your overpayment falls within the penalty-free annual allowance (typically 10% of the outstanding balance).
          </p>
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] text-xs text-slate-400 space-y-2">
            <p><strong>Official Reference:</strong> <a href="https://www.moneyhelper.org.uk/en/homes/buying-a-home/mortgage-overpayments" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[#00F5D4] hover:underline font-medium">MoneyHelper — Mortgage Overpayments →</a></p>
            <p class="italic text-slate-500">Calculations are estimates. Consult an FCA-regulated mortgage adviser for personalised guidance on overpayments and remortgaging.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Related Calculators Section -->
      <section class="space-y-4 pt-4">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">Related Calculators & Tools</h3>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
          <a href="/capital-gains-tax-calculator/uk/" class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] hover:border-[#00F5D4]/40 transition-all space-y-2 group">
            <span class="text-xs font-bold text-[#00F5D4] uppercase">UK Tax Planning</span>
            <h4 class="text-base font-bold text-white group-hover:text-[#00F5D4] transition-colors">UK Capital Gains Tax Calculator</h4>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Estimate CGT on property disposals factoring in HMRC Annual Exempt Amounts and income band rates.</p>
          </a>
          <a href="/buy-borrow-die-calculator/uk/" class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] hover:border-[#7B61FF]/40 transition-all space-y-2 group">
            <span class="text-xs font-bold text-[#7B61FF] uppercase">UK Wealth Strategy</span>
            <h4 class="text-base font-bold text-white group-hover:text-[#7B61FF] transition-colors">UK Buy, Borrow, Die Calculator</h4>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Model borrowing against appreciated UK portfolios to avoid triggering CGT disposal events.</p>
          </a>
          <a href="/tax-loss-harvesting-calculator/uk/" class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] hover:border-[#00F5D4]/40 transition-all space-y-2 group">
            <span class="text-xs font-bold text-[#00F5D4] uppercase">UK Loss Offsetting</span>
            <h4 class="text-base font-bold text-white group-hover:text-[#00F5D4] transition-colors">UK Tax-Loss Harvesting Calculator</h4>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Calculate how allowable HMRC capital losses reduce chargeable capital gains.</p>
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
